  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Maritime security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In FY2023-24, the Philippine Civil Maritime Security Program (PCMSP) provided technical assistance to Philippine policy makers grappling with maritime security sector policy reform. This work supported the landmark Maritime Zones Bill, which will provide a framework for negotiations between South China Sea claimant states. PCMSP held four seminars, commissioned two surveys and published 24 papers on topical civil maritime security issues. In 2023, Australia ranked second among international partners in a nationwide Philippine civil maritime security and protection awareness poll (one place higher than in 2022).</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">PCMSP is supporting the Philippines to address marine environmental issues. Two coral restoration projects are helping coastal communities in Zambales, Palawan, Pangasinan and Cebu to adapt to the economic and environmental impacts of climate change. Providing science-based solutions to these communities, whose livelihoods depend on healthy coral reef systems for fishing, builds community-level understanding and support for sustainability measures.
&#x200d;
PCMSP is also supporting the Philippines to reduce marine litter through improved litter monitoring and mitigation practices. This project has conducted three training and data collection exercises and completed nearly 1,000 surveys across hundreds of coastal, inland and riverine sites. This marine litter project will conduct the first baseline of plastics in riverine, coastal and land sites in Philippine tourist destinations. The data set generated will contribute to broader global studies and support large scale predictions about local, national, regional, and global debris losses into the environment.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Philippine Civil Maritime Security Program (PCMSP) delivered marine litter training to 25 participants from Central Visayas local government units (LGUs) to quantify the amount of litter form the islands of Cebu and Bohol in the Philippines. Over 9,400 litter items were detected and recorded across the 94 sites surveyed. A total of 311 transects (a path that cuts through a natural landscape along which objects are studied) were conducted, including 84 coastal transects, 113 inland transects, and 88 river transects. The data collected provides the fourth comprehensive baseline assessment of plastics and other anthropogenic litter on land, along rivers and at the coastal interface for such a large portion of Philippines. 
&#x200d;
Further, PCMSP provided expert technical assistance to 20 Philippine Coast Guard officials to complement the Philippine Government response to oil spills.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Maritime security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Philippine Civil Maritime Security Program (PCMSP) delivered maritime security training, engaging 50 participants from government agencies. PCMSP launched two fellowship programs &#x2014;for 37 marine scientists and policymakers, and for emerging leaders in maritime governance. These programs are designed to cultivate a new generation of leaders committed to advancing maritime reform. PCMSP also supported the formation of the Archipelagic Women of the Philippines, a network of 30 women leaders across the country. Established to amplify women&#x2019;s voices in maritime affairs, the network advances a shared agenda for environmental protection, improved livelihoods and maritime leadership. 
&#x200d;
PCMSP was also instrumental in passing the Archipelagic Sea Lanes Act, strategically framing it alongside the Maritime Zones Act as essential for the Philippines&apos; maritime governance under the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS). This work provided technical support, such as harmonising relevant provisions in the two bills as they progressed through bicameral approval in July and September 2024. 
&#x200d;
In December 2024 Australia ranked third among international partners in a nationwide Philippine civil maritime security and protection awareness poll (one place higher than in June 2024).</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
